==English==
{{was wotd|2018|June|7}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-|*wert-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|sco|stalwart}} under the influence of {{w|Walter Scott}}, displacing earlier {{doublet|en|stalworth|notext=1}}, wherewith it forms a doublet. From {{der|en|enm|stal-worth|t=physically strong, hardy, robust; brave, courageous}}, from {{der|en|ang|stǣlwierþe|t=able to stand in good stead, serviceable}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=stal-worth|pos=adj|id=MED42606|accessdate=13 March 2018}}</ref> probably from {{m|ang|staþol|t=establishment; foundation}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-|t=to stand (up)}}) or {{m|ang|stǣl|t=place; condition, stead}} + {{m|ang|-wierþe|t=''suffix meaning ‘able to, capable of’''}} (probably ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wert-|t=to rotate, turn}}).

